---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { CLOCKS } from '../../data/clocks';
import { LICENSES } from '../../data/licenses';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';
import { STATES } from '../../data/states';

const citedClocks = CLOCKS.flatMap((clock) => {
  const state = STATES.find((s) => s.code === clock.state);
  const license = LICENSES.find((l) => l.id === clock.license);
  const label = clock.label.trim();
  if (!state || !license || !label) return [];
  return [{ clock, state, license, label }];
});

const moneyTransmitterClocks = citedClocks.filter(
  (row) => row.license.id === 'money-transmitter',
);
const day120 = moneyTransmitterClocks.find((row) => row.clock.days === 120)?.clock.days;

const lmsDirectory = `${SITE.sisters.simple.url}/directory`;
const storeCompare = `${SITE.sisters.store.url}/compare`;
const navigatorUrl = 'https://licensenavigator.ai/';
const suretyUrl = 'https://suretyresearch.com/';
---

<Layout
  title="How long does a business license take | FastLicense"
  description="A business license clock usually starts when the file is complete, not when you click submit. Read the cited state page."
>
  <section class="masthead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="crumbs">
        <a href="/">Home</a> / Guide
      </p>
      <p class="kicker">Guide</p>
      <h1>How long does a business license take?</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        There is no single wait for a U.S. business license. Open the live state and license page on
        FastLicense and read the citation for the license you will file.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>
        The page prints a figure only when a statute or a regulator page publishes one. If a typical
        range is unpublished, the cell stays blank on purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        FastLicense and the sister licensing surfaces (
        <a href={lmsDirectory} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{SITE.sisters.simple.name}</a>,
        <a href={storeCompare} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{SITE.sisters.store.name}</a>,
        <a href={navigatorUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Licensing Navigator</a>, and
        <a href={suretyUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">SuretyResearch</a>) sit in the same owner
        network as <a href={SITE.cornerstone.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{SITE.cornerstone.name}</a>.
        This guide covers the timeline method. The other surfaces cover activity, cost, license type, and bonds.
      </p>

      <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Most statutory clocks start when the application is complete, not when you click submit.</li>
        <li>A statutory deadline is not an average processing time.</li>
        <li>NMLS is the filing channel on many of these licenses. The state keeps the review clock.</li>
        <li>
          FastLicense prints a number only when a statute or regulator page publishes it. A blank means
          that range is unpublished.
        </li>
        <li>Read the state page, then ask Cornerstone if you need the file sequenced on a real calendar.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>1. FastLicense</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/">FastLicense</a> is where you read cited clocks for U.S. collection, consumer lending,
        money transmission, and mortgage company filings.
      </p>
      <p>
        A figure shows up when a statute or a regulator page publishes it. A blank means the typical
        range is unpublished. Do not read a blank as zero days or as a fast file. The source did not
        give a range worth repeating.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site publishes a small set of cited clocks. Open the live page for your state and license
        and keep its label. This list is that set. A clock with no published label stays off the list.
      </p>
      {
        citedClocks.length > 0 ? (
          <ul>
            {citedClocks.map(({ clock, state, license, label }) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/licenses/${license.slug}/${state.slug}/`}>
                  {state.name} {license.shortName}
                </a>
                {clock.adjacent ? ' (adjacent product). ' : '. '}
                {label}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        ) : (
          <p>This build has no cited clock with a published label. The typical range stays blank.</p>
        )
      }
      <p>
        Every other pair stays unpublished. Start from <a href="/licenses/">license types</a> or the
        <a href="/states/"> state index</a>, then open the page for the filing you mean. If the cell is
        blank, leave it blank.
      </p>

      <h3>Completeness starts the clock</h3>
      <p>
        The clock that matters starts when the application is complete. Clicking submit only means the
        channel took the package.
      </p>
      <p>
        Complete means the statute or the regulator checklist is satisfied. A missing owner disclosure,
        a bond that is not yet in force, or an open deficiency leaves the file incomplete. The review
        period often has not started, even after a fee clears.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> states what FastLicense will cite and why a blank stays
        blank. Read it before you put a number on a launch plan.
      </p>

      <h3>A deadline is not an average</h3>
      <p>
        A statute can require the regulator to act within a set number of days after a complete
        application. That is a deadline, meaning a duty to act. It is not an average, and it is not
        approval on the last day.
      </p>
      <p>
        {
          day120 ? (
            <>
              When a money transmitter page cites {day120} days on a complete application statute, keep
              that label.
            </>
          ) : (
            <>When a money transmitter page cites a complete application statute, keep that label.</>
          )
        }{' '}
        Approval, denial, and a request for more items are different results.
      </p>

      <h3>NMLS is the channel. The state keeps the clock</h3>
      <p>
        Many of these filings move through NMLS. NMLS is the channel for forms and uploads. The state
        reviews the license and owns the clock.
      </p>
      <p>
        A submitted status does not prove the statutory period has started. Deficiencies, background
        checks, and surety items can leave the file short of the state's completeness test.
      </p>

      <h3>What actually burns calendar time</h3>
      <p>
        Most lost days happen before the official clock starts. Missing exhibits, an unplaced bond, a
        control-person issue, or an unanswered deficiency will stall the file. The
        <a href="/delays/">delays</a> page lists those patterns and does not assign a day count to each.
      </p>
      <p>
        Expedite exists only where the state offers it. See the <a href="/expedite/">expedite</a> page.
        Nobody can sell a shorter review than the regulator created. Cleaner preparation can still get
        the file to complete sooner.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Who needs which license</h2>
      <p>
        Match the activity first. A clock for one license does not carry over to a different activity
        in the same state.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={lmsDirectory} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{SITE.sisters.simple.name}</a> is the
        directory for who needs what. Collection, consumer lending, money transmission, and mortgage
        company authority are different permissions. A complete file on the wrong license does not start
        the clock you care about.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Cost and who can file</h2>
      <p>
        Price does not set the state's clock. A missing fee can still block completeness, the same way
        a missing form can.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={storeCompare} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{SITE.sisters.store.name}</a> compares
        providers and commercial cost. Use it to see who can prepare the file and what the offer covers.
        It is not a speed ranking. Cited fees, when a source publishes them, belong on the state page.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Which license type you mean</h2>
      <p>
        Say which license you mean before you ask for a timeline. A city general business license does
        not share a calendar with a state collection, lending, mortgage, or money transmission license.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={navigatorUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Licensing Navigator</a> sorts license type
        when the activity could sit in more than one regime. Come back to FastLicense for the cited
        clock on the financial license. This guide does not cite city or county clerk calendars.
      </p>

      <h2>5. When a bond sits in the path</h2>
      <p>
        A required bond often has to be in force before the application counts as complete. Until the
        regulator can see that security, the statutory review clock may not be running.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={suretyUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">SuretyResearch</a> covers underwriting,
        indemnity, and the state's bond form. Those steps sit outside the statute's days to act
        sentence. Do not add a guessed bond interval to a cited deadline. If no page publishes a
        combined figure, you do not have one.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Ask Cornerstone to sequence a real file</h2>
      <p>
        A cited clock frames the law. It does not build your calendar.
        <a href={SITE.cornerstone.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{SITE.cornerstone.name}</a>
        sequences a real file once you know the state, the license, and whether a bond is in the path.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cornerstone's published track record is more than 28 years and over 525,000 filings, with
        99.995% on-time submissions in 2025. On-time submission is filing discipline. It is not
        state approval inside a statutory window. The regulator still controls review.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keep three dates separate. The day the channel accepts the file. The day the file is complete
        under the state's test. The day the statute, if FastLicense cites one, says the regulator must act.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn btn-solid" href={SITE.cornerstone.contact} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ask Cornerstone</a>
      </p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does the clock start when I click submit?</summary>
          <p>
            Usually no. On most of these licenses the statutory period starts when the application is
            complete. Submit means the channel accepted the package. Check the citation on your state
            and license page before your team counts days.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why is the timeline blank for my state?</summary>
          <p>
            The blank is intentional. A figure appears only when a statute or regulator page publishes
            it. Do not copy another state's number. Open the
            <a href="/licenses/">license</a> and <a href="/states/">state</a> pages and keep the cell
            that is already there.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is 120 days the normal wait for a money transmitter license?</summary>
          <p>
            No. A deadline on a statute is not a national average and not approval on the last day.
            {
              day120 ? (
                <>
                  Some money transmitter pages in this directory cite {day120} days after a complete
                  application. Read the state page that carries that label.
                </>
              ) : (
                <>This directory does not currently cite a 120 day money transmitter clock.</>
              )
            }
          </p>
          {
            moneyTransmitterClocks.length > 0 && (
              <ul>
                {moneyTransmitterClocks.map(({ state, license, label }) => (
                  <li>
                    <a href={`/licenses/${license.slug}/${state.slug}/`}>{state.name}</a>. {label}
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            )
          }
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does NMLS decide how long approval takes?</summary>
          <p>
            No. NMLS is the filing channel for many of these applications. The state keeps the review
            clock. Channel status and the state's completeness test are separate facts. Track both.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I expedite a collection, lending, mortgage, or money transmission file?</summary>
          <p>
            Only where that state offers a faster path. Read <a href="/expedite/">expedite</a> with the
            state page. A firm cannot compress a review the regulator did not create. You can still
            shorten the time it takes to reach a complete file.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if a bond has to land before review starts?</summary>
          <p>
            Treat the bond as a completeness item. While it is missing, the review clock may not be
            running. Read <a href={suretyUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">SuretyResearch</a> for the
            bond path, then ask Cornerstone to sequence bond steps with the license clock.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <h2>Next step</h2>
      <p>
        Open the state and license page on <a href="/">FastLicense</a> for the activity you will
        conduct. Read the cited figure or keep the blank. Use <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>,
        <a href="/delays/">delays</a>, and <a href="/expedite/">expedite</a> so your start date is
        completeness, not the upload click.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you need a working calendar for a real file,
        <a href={SITE.cornerstone.contact} target="_blank" rel="noopener">ask Cornerstone</a> to
        sequence it. Bring the state, the license, and whether a bond sits in the path. Still matching
        activity to permission? Use the
        <a href={lmsDirectory} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{SITE.sisters.simple.name} directory</a>.
        Comparing who can file and commercial cost? Use
        <a href={storeCompare} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{SITE.sisters.store.name}</a>. Unsure of
        the license type? Use <a href={navigatorUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Licensing Navigator</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
